      "literal_source_text": "D.1 Hilbert space and Markov operator conventions Definition D.1 (Hilbert space notation) . Let \\zeta ζ \\zeta be a probability measure. The real Hilbert space L^{2}(\\zeta) L 2 ​ ( ζ ) L^{2}(\\zeta) consists of equivalence classes of measurable functions f f f such that \\int f^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta<\\infty ∫ f 2 ​ 𝑑 ζ < ∞ \\int f^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta<\\infty . Its inner product and norm are \\langle f,g\\rangle_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}\\coloneqq\\int fg\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\|f\\|_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}\\coloneqq\\langle f,f\\rangle_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}^{1/2}\\,. ⟨ f , g ⟩ L 2 ​ ( ζ ) ≔ ∫ f ​ g ​ 𝑑 ζ , ‖ f ‖ L 2 ​ ( ζ ) ≔ ⟨ f , f ⟩ L 2 ​ ( ζ ) 1 / 2 . \\langle f,g\\rangle_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}\\coloneqq\\int fg\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\|f\\|_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}\\coloneqq\\langle f,f\\rangle_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}^{1/2}\\,. (D.1) We write L_{0}^{2}(\\zeta)\\coloneqq\\Bigl\\{f\\in L^{2}(\\zeta):\\int f\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta=0\\Bigr\\}\\,. L 0 2 ​ ( ζ ) ≔ { f ∈ L 2 ​ ( ζ ) : ∫ f ​ 𝑑 ζ = 0 } . L_{0}^{2}(\\zeta)\\coloneqq\\Bigl\\{f\\in L^{2}(\\zeta):\\int f\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta=0\\Bigr\\}\\,. (D.2) When \\zeta=\\pi_{\\eta} ζ = π η \\zeta=\\pi_{\\eta} , we abbreviate the inner product and norm by \\langle f,g\\rangle ⟨ f , g ⟩ \\langle f,g\\rangle and \\|f\\| ‖ f ‖ \\|f\\| . Two functions equal \\zeta ζ \\zeta -almost everywhere represent the same vector. A closed linear subspace \\mathcal{M}\\subseteq L^{2}(\\zeta) ℳ ⊆ L 2 ​ ( ζ ) \\mathcal{M}\\subseteq L^{2}(\\zeta) has orthogonal complement \\mathcal{M}^{\\perp}\\coloneqq\\{g:\\langle f,g\\rangle=0\\text{ for all }f\\in\\mathcal{M}\\} ℳ ⟂ ≔ { g : ⟨ f , g ⟩ = 0 ​ for all ​ f ∈ ℳ } \\mathcal{M}^{\\perp}\\coloneqq\\{g:\\langle f,g\\rangle=0\\text{ for all }f\\in\\mathcal{M}\\} . Its orthogonal projection is the unique bounded operator \\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}} 𝖯 ℳ \\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}} for which \\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}}f\\in\\mathcal{M} 𝖯 ℳ ​ f ∈ ℳ \\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}}f\\in\\mathcal{M} and f-\\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}}f\\in\\mathcal{M}^{\\perp} f − 𝖯 ℳ ​ f ∈ ℳ ⟂ f-\\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}}f\\in\\mathcal{M}^{\\perp} . All operators in the convergence analysis are bounded linear operators on the indicated Hilbert spaces unless explicitly stated otherwise. The adjoint \\mathsf{L}^{*} 𝖫 ∗ \\mathsf{L}^{*} is characterized by \\langle\\mathsf{L}f,g\\rangle=\\langle f,\\mathsf{L}^{*}g\\rangle ⟨ 𝖫 ​ f , g ⟩ = ⟨ f , 𝖫 ∗ ​ g ⟩ \\langle\\mathsf{L}f,g\\rangle=\\langle f,\\mathsf{L}^{*}g\\rangle . An operator is self-adjoint if \\mathsf{L}=\\mathsf{L}^{*} 𝖫 = 𝖫 ∗ \\mathsf{L}=\\mathsf{L}^{*} , a contraction if \\|\\mathsf{L}f\\|\\leq\\|f\\| ‖ 𝖫 ​ f ‖ ≤ ‖ f ‖ \\|\\mathsf{L}f\\|\\leq\\|f\\| , and unitary if it is onto and preserves norms. A self-adjoint involution, meaning \\mathsf{L}^{2}=I 𝖫 2 = I \\mathsf{L}^{2}=I , is unitary. For self-adjoint operators \\mathsf{L}_{1},\\mathsf{L}_{2} 𝖫 1 , 𝖫 2 \\mathsf{L}_{1},\\mathsf{L}_{2} , \\mathsf{L}_{1}\\preceq\\mathsf{L}_{2}\\hskip 10.00002pt\\Longleftrightarrow\\hskip 10.00002pt\\langle f,(\\mathsf{L}_{2}-\\mathsf{L}_{1})f\\rangle\\geq 0\\hskip 10.00002pt\\text{for every }f 𝖫 1 ⪯ 𝖫 2 ⟺ ⟨ f , ( 𝖫 2 − 𝖫 1 ) f ⟩ ≥ 0 for every f \\mathsf{L}_{1}\\preceq\\mathsf{L}_{2}\\hskip 10.00002pt\\Longleftrightarrow\\hskip 10.00002pt\\langle f,(\\mathsf{L}_{2}-\\mathsf{L}_{1})f\\rangle\\geq 0\\hskip 10.00002pt\\text{for every }f (D.3) is the Loewner order. If \\mathsf{L} 𝖫 \\mathsf{L} is self-adjoint, the spectral theorem defines \\psi(\\mathsf{L}) ψ ⁡ ( 𝖫 ) \\psi(\\mathsf{L}) for bounded Borel functions \\psi ψ \\psi ; in particular \\mathbf{1}_{E}(\\mathsf{L}) 𝟏 E ​ ( 𝖫 ) \\mathbf{1}_{E}(\\mathsf{L}) is the orthogonal projection onto the spectral set E E E , and a non-negative operator has a unique non-negative square root. Definition D.2 (Markov operators and density evolution) . Let \\mathcal{K}(z,\\mathrm{d}z^{\\prime}) 𝒦 ⁡ ( z , d ​ z ′ ) \\mathcal{K}(z,\\mathrm{d}z^{\\prime}) be a Markov kernel preserving \\zeta ζ \\zeta . Its Markov operator on observables is (\\mathsf{K}f)(z)\\coloneqq\\int f(z^{\\prime})\\,\\mathcal{K}(z,\\mathrm{d}z^{\\prime})\\,. ( 𝖪 ​ f ) ​ ( z ) ≔ ∫ f ⁡ ( z ′ ) ​ 𝒦 ​ ( z , d ​ z ′ ) . (\\mathsf{K}f)(z)\\coloneqq\\int f(z^{\\prime})\\,\\mathcal{K}(z,\\mathrm{d}z^{\\prime})\\,. (D.4) It is a contraction on L^{2}(\\zeta) L 2 ​ ( ζ ) L^{2}(\\zeta) . Its adjoint \\mathsf{K}^{*} 𝖪 ∗ \\mathsf{K}^{*} governs density evolution: if a law has density h h h with respect to \\zeta ζ \\zeta , then its density after one step is \\mathsf{K}^{*}h 𝖪 ∗ ​ h \\mathsf{K}^{*}h . Constants are fixed by both operators. If P\\ll\\zeta P ≪ ζ P\\ll\\zeta has density h h h , then \\chi^{2}(P\\|\\zeta)=\\|h-1\\|_{\\zeta}^{2}\\,. χ 2 ( P ∥ ζ ) = ∥ h − 1 ∥ ζ 2 . \\chi^{2}(P\\|\\zeta)=\\|h-1\\|_{\\zeta}^{2}\\,. (D.5) Consequently, an operator norm bound for \\mathsf{K} 𝖪 \\mathsf{K} on L_{0}^{2}(\\zeta) L 0 2 ​ ( ζ ) L_{0}^{2}(\\zeta) gives the same bound for its adjoint \\mathsf{K}^{*} 𝖪 ∗ \\mathsf{K}^{*} , and hence gives \\chi^{2} χ 2 \\chi^{2} contraction."
