      "literal_source_text": "Our target distribution is the joint law \\pi_{\\eta} π η \\pi_{\\eta} defined in ( 2.6 ), and we set \\mathfrak{H}\\coloneqq L^{2}(\\pi_{\\eta}) ℌ ≔ L 2 ​ ( π η ) \\mathfrak{H}\\coloneqq L^{2}(\\pi_{\\eta}) . Recall from ( 3.11 ) that the ideal Proximal BPS has transition kernel \\mathcal{K}\\bigl((x,y),\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime}\\bigr)=\\delta_{2x-y}(\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime})\\,\\bigl[\\rho\\,\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y^{\\prime}}(\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})+(1-\\rho)\\,\\mathcal{H}_{y^{\\prime}}(x,\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})\\bigr]\\,, 𝒦 ⁡ ( ( x , y ) , d ​ x ′ ​ d ​ y ′ ) = δ 2 ​ x − y ​ ( d ​ y ′ ) ​ [ ρ ​ π η X | Y = y ′ ​ ( d ​ x ′ ) + ( 1 − ρ ) ​ ℋ y ′ ​ ( x , d ​ x ′ ) ] , \\mathcal{K}\\bigl((x,y),\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime}\\bigr)=\\delta_{2x-y}(\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime})\\,\\bigl[\\rho\\,\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y^{\\prime}}(\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})+(1-\\rho)\\,\\mathcal{H}_{y^{\\prime}}(x,\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})\\bigr]\\,, where \\mathcal{H}_{y^{\\prime}} ℋ y ′ \\mathcal{H}_{y^{\\prime}} is the conditional half-turn kernel from Proposition 3.2 . Since the algorithm is a discrete-time chain, its evolution is described by the induced one-step Markov operator on observables, (\\mathsf{K}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^{2d}}f(x^{\\prime},y^{\\prime})\\,\\mathcal{K}\\bigl((x,y),\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime}\\bigr)\\,. ( 𝖪 ​ f ) ​ ( x , y ) ≔ ∫ ℝ 2 ​ d f ⁡ ( x ′ , y ′ ) ​ 𝒦 ​ ( ( x , y ) , d ​ x ′ ​ d ​ y ′ ) . (\\mathsf{K}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^{2d}}f(x^{\\prime},y^{\\prime})\\,\\mathcal{K}\\bigl((x,y),\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime}\\bigr)\\,. Thus \\mathcal{K} 𝒦 \\mathcal{K} acts on probability laws, whereas \\mathsf{K} 𝖪 \\mathsf{K} is its induced action on observables. The first term in brackets in \\mathcal{K} 𝒦 \\mathcal{K} is the conditional resampling branch. After the auxiliary coordinate is updated to y^{\\prime} y ′ y^{\\prime} , this branch replaces the x x x -coordinate by an independent draw from \\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y^{\\prime}} π η X | Y = y ′ \\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y^{\\prime}} . The associated conditional expectation operator is (\\mathsf{P}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq\\int f(x^{\\prime},y)\\,\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y}(\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})=\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y=y]\\,. ( 𝖯 ​ f ) ​ ( x , y ) ≔ ∫ f ⁡ ( x ′ , y ) ​ π η X | Y = y ​ ( d ​ x ′ ) = 𝔼 ⁡ [ f ⁡ ( X , Y ) ∣ Y = y ] . (\\mathsf{P}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq\\int f(x^{\\prime},y)\\,\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y}(\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})=\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y=y]\\,. (B.1) In particular, \\mathsf{P}f 𝖯 ​ f \\mathsf{P}f depends only on the coordinate y y y . The range and kernel of \\mathsf{P} 𝖯 \\mathsf{P} are \\begin{split}\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}&\\coloneqq\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:f(x,y)=g(y)\\text{ for some }g\\in L^{2}(\\pi_{\\eta}^{Y})\\}\\,,\\\\ \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp}&\\coloneqq\\ker(\\mathsf{P})=\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y]=0\\}\\,.\\end{split} ℌ 𝖯 ≔ ran ⁡ ( 𝖯 ) = { f ∈ ℌ : f ⁡ ( x , y ) = g ⁡ ( y ) ​ for some ​ g ∈ L 2 ​ ( π η Y ) } , ℌ ⟂ ≔ ker ⁡ ( 𝖯 ) = ran ⁡ ( 𝖯 ⟂ ) = { f ∈ ℌ : 𝔼 ⁡ [ f ⁡ ( X , Y ) ∣ Y ] = 0 } . \\begin{split}\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}&\\coloneqq\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:f(x,y)=g(y)\\text{ for some }g\\in L^{2}(\\pi_{\\eta}^{Y})\\}\\,,\\\\ \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp}&\\coloneqq\\ker(\\mathsf{P})=\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y]=0\\}\\,.\\end{split} (B.2) Here \\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\coloneqq I-\\mathsf{P} 𝖯 ⟂ ≔ I − 𝖯 \\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\coloneqq I-\\mathsf{P} , so \\mathfrak{H}=\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\oplus\\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} ℌ = ℌ 𝖯 ⊕ ℌ ⟂ \\mathfrak{H}=\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\oplus\\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} and f=\\mathsf{P}f+\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}f f = 𝖯 ​ f + 𝖯 ⟂ ​ f f=\\mathsf{P}f+\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}f . In the common terminology of hypocoercivity [ 14 , 21 ] , \\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P}) ran ⁡ ( 𝖯 ) \\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P}) is the macroscopic subspace and its orthogonal complement is the microscopic subspace. Thus functions in \\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}} ℌ 𝖯 \\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}} depend only on the coordinate y y y , whereas functions in \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} ℌ ⟂ \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} have zero mean under \\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y} π η X | Y = y \\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y} for \\pi_{\\eta}^{Y} π η Y \\pi_{\\eta}^{Y} -almost every y y y ."
